-- title:
--   Theorem 4.1 — connectivity in the continuum
-- statement:
--   Let $E$ be a finite-dimensional real inner product space and $\Gamma$ a
--   configuration on it with a uniform positive lower bound $\vartheta$ on its apex
--   angles. Let $U \subseteq E$ be open and preconnected, and let $x, y \in U$.
--   Then $x$ and $y$ are joined by a finite cone chain: there are
--   $x = z_0, z_1, \dots, z_n = y$ in $E$ with $n \ge 0$ such that for each $i$,
--
--   $$\bigl(z_i \in U \ \text{and}\ z_{i+1} \in V^\Gamma[z_i]\bigr)
--   \quad\text{or}\quad
--   \bigl(z_{i+1} \in U \ \text{and}\ z_i \in V^\Gamma[z_{i+1}]\bigr).$$
--
--   **Each step needs only *one* of its endpoints in $U$.** An intermediate $z_i$
--   may therefore lie outside $U$, provided both its neighbours lie in $U$ and see
--   it in their cones. So this is not a statement about chains staying inside $U$;
--   the development's `ConnWithin`, which does require that, is a different
--   relation and is not the one used here.
--
--   No bound on $n$ is claimed, in terms of $\vartheta$, $\operatorname{diam} U$,
--   $\|x-y\|$ or $\dim E$ — only that some finite chain exists. The case $n = 0$ is
--   allowed, so $x = y$ is trivial. Only $\vartheta$-boundedness is assumed; no
--   measurability of $\Gamma$ enters, and $\vartheta$ itself does not appear in the
--   conclusion — its role is to make the lower bound on apex angles uniform in $x$.
